<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#flag-of-denmark">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flag of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dannebrog</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the Dannebrog, a white Scandinavian cross on a red field, is often said to be the oldest national flag in continuous use. Legend, first written down in the 16th century, says it fell from heaven during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise">Battle of Lindanise</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in 1219, but the red-and-white cross is first securely attested as a Danish royal emblem in the 14th century. Its design inspired the crosses of the other Nordic flags.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flag-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Flag of Denmark" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madden and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Denmark.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The white-on-red cross emblem dates from the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>. The earliest known undisputed colour rendering of the Dannebrog is in the Gelre Armorial, of about 1340 to 1370, which shows such a banner beside the arms of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Valdemar IV displayed a cross in his arms on a seal of 1356. Why the kings began to display it is unknown. Caspar Paludan-Müller suggested a banner sent by the pope during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a>, and Adolf Ditlev Jørgensen the banner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>.</p><p>The origin legend was written down in the 1520s by Christiern Pedersen and the friar Petrus Olai. In Olai's most influential version the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> under Valdemar II were close to defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise">Battle of Lindanise</a>, near Tallinn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, on 15 June 1219. Bishop Anders Sunesen prayed on a hill with his arms raised, and the Danes advanced while they stayed up, a story almost identical to Exodus 17. As defeat threatened, the flag fell from the sky, the king showed it to the troops, and they won. Other versions place the event at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Fellin</a> in 1208.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Dannebrog, meaning red cloth, is a red flag with a white Nordic cross extending to the edges, its vertical arm shifted towards the hoist. A regulation of 1748, still in force, sets the cross at one seventh of the flag's height. No official definition of the red exists. The swallow-tailed Splitflag is used on land, and a deeper red Orlogsflag at sea by the navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>According to tradition the original flag was lost at the Battle of Hemmingstedt on 17 February 1500 and recaptured by Frederik II in 1559, though contemporary records of the battle do not mention its loss. It was used as a maritime flag from the sixteenth century, and became the flag of the whole army from 1842. In the early nineteenth century, with Romantic nationalism, it came to be seen as representing the nation. A circular of 7 January 1834 outlawed its private use, and a regulation of 7 July 1854 permitted it, after the national enthusiasm of the First Schleswig War. It is often said to be the oldest national flag in continuous use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dannebrog” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Valdemar II “the Victorious” led a crusade to Estonia, where, according to a legend first recorded in the sixteenth century, the <b>Dannebrog</b> flag fell from heaven during the Battle of Lyndanisse in 1219.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1219 King Valdemar II of Denmark landed with a crusading army, defeated the Estonians at the Battle of Lindanise, the battle at which, according to later legend, the Danish flag, the <b>Dannebrog</b>, fell from heaven, and built a castle on Toompea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Legend holds that the Danish flag, the <b>Dannebrog</b>, fell from the sky during the battle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1219 Valdemar II of Denmark landed with a crusading army, defeated the Estonians at the Battle of Lindanise and built a castle on Toompea; legend places the falling of the Danish flag, the <b>Dannebrog</b>, from the sky at this battle.</blockquote><a href="/state/ravala">Rävala</a></li></ul></section>
